And we'll make adjustments in between.

00:31:33.620 --> 00:31:36.340
We know about wedge, changing the weight on each tire.

00:31:37.000 --> 00:31:38.300
We know about pannered rods.

00:31:38.770 --> 00:31:40.550
Lowering the pannered rod makes the car tighter.

00:31:40.910 --> 00:31:42.650
Raising the pannered rod makes the car looser.

00:31:44.340 --> 00:31:45.540
Also use tire pressure.

00:31:46.180 --> 00:31:48.440
Dropping the tire pressure or raising the tire pressure

00:31:48.440 --> 00:31:50.980
by as little as one pound can make a big difference

00:31:50.980 --> 00:31:52.000
in the tire's spring rate.

00:31:54.320 --> 00:31:55.360
We'll use spring rubbers.

00:31:56.240 --> 00:31:57.880
Putting a piece of rubber in a spring

00:31:57.880 --> 00:31:59.650
or pulling it out of a spring.

00:32:00.280 --> 00:32:03.880
can make that spring stiffer or softer by as much as 25%.

00:32:03.880 --> 00:32:08.240
But what's really different about this year than all the years past?

00:32:08.820 --> 00:32:10.740
Well, NASCAR's made an aerodynamic rule change.

00:32:10.990 --> 00:32:15.070
They've made us take the fenders from 76 inches wide to 74 inches wide.

00:32:15.150 --> 00:32:17.210
That's taking front downforce away from the cars.

00:32:17.700 --> 00:32:22.930
To get that downforce back, you're going to see a lot of guys putting more and more tape on as the night goes on.

00:32:23.380 --> 00:32:26.140
The more tape goes on, the more engine temperature comes up.

00:32:26.400 --> 00:32:29.650
The more engine temperature comes up, the bigger your chances are of blowing up.

00:32:30.250 --> 00:32:31.290
How important is that tape?

00:32:31.590 --> 00:32:35.670
Jeff Gordon went from second to fifth in Talladega when one strip of tape came off.

